in just four weeks the number of  coronavirus cases  in children soared 90 percent in the u.s  the american academy of pediatrics  reports almost  180 000 new cases in kids  between july 9th and august 6th based on  data from states  more than 380 thousand covid 19 cases in  children  have been reported in this country since  the pandemic began  senior medical correspondent dr tara  narula joins us now tara good morning  what does the data say about how the  virus affects  kids well anthony you know parents are  really struggling with what to do  particularly when it comes to schools  and so we need to give them a realistic  understanding  of what the data and the science says  and for now the understanding is that  overwhelmingly  kids tend to be affected less severely  there are low rates of hospitalization  for kids  and when you look at the states in new  york city that reported mortality data  kids have accounted for about zero to  0.4  of the covid deaths in those that  reported now it's important to  say that when we hear words like kids  are immune  those words matter and words like immune  are misleading  and inaccurate when you think about the  fact that nine percent of all the  coveted cases in this country  are in kids that kids have been  hospitalized  and the 86 children that have died to  hear  those words that kids are immune is  extremely disheartening for those  parents that have suffered those losses  tara what's behind this recent surge in  cases in kids  well certainly anthony we're doing a lot  more testing now than we were in the  beginning  so we are going to pick up on more kids  we're now testing not just the sickest  of the sick  we're testing kids who may be mildly  symptomatic  but also the data is really generated  from the states  and so it depends on how the states are  reporting the data what they're  releasing publicly what format they're  reporting that data in  and even how they classify child cases  varies from state to state some states  say  it's 0 to 14 other states say it's 0 to  24. so  we're really getting an emerging but  incomplete picture  of what this looks like for children in  this country you  you mentioned the issue of kids going  back to school it's something  millions of parents are confronting  right now what does this tell us about  the safety of classrooms  well i think first of all there's no  question it's polarizing and we've seen  success stories both internationally and  even in this country when you look at  certain day care centers and camps  we've also seen failures but in general  i think that schools can reopen  in certain places safely and i think  most public health officials will tell  you the priorities should be to open  schools but it really depends on three  things  first of all the infection rate the  community transmission rate in that  area of the school has to be low the  school itself  has to make sure that they're putting  into place really strong precautions  like  having kids in small pods social  distancing masks  good ventilation isolation and testing  and tracing  and then finally families really have to  look at who their  child is are is their child more  vulnerable do they have an underlying  health condition is someone in their  family more vulnerable  and then when we talk about teachers too  we need to make sure that we  are calling them frontline workers and  providing them with things like ppe  but i think it can be done in the right  setting in the right places  but there's not a one-size-fits-all  approach for every part of the country  dr taran arula thank you very much
 

